## Summary

Proactive hardening: provider HTTP clients used Go's default redirect policy, so a 3xx from a configured upstream would silently re-issue the call — with its credential and the installation's `forwarded_client_headers` — against whatever host the `Location` named. A provider base URL is configuration, not discovery, so there is no case where a redirect target is a host we intended to authenticate to.

New shared constructor in `providers/httputil`, wired into every provider client (anthropic, openai, openaicompat, google native, cortexagents), replacing the `&http.Client{Transport: ...}` literals:

```go
func NewClient(transport http.RoundTripper) *http.Client {
	return &http.Client{Transport: transport, CheckRedirect: refuseRedirect}
}

func refuseRedirect(*http.Request, []*http.Request) error {
	return http.ErrUseLastResponse
}
```

`ErrUseLastResponse` (the same policy `policyclient` and the backend's model-list client already use) hands the 3xx back to the caller unfollowed rather than erroring, so the response/error classification paths (`IsRetryable`, upstream error envelopes) see a normal non-2xx. Transports, timeouts, pooling, and HTTP/2 config are untouched — this only changes what happens on a redirect. Adapter recipe in `providers/{CLAUDE,AGENTS}.md` updated to point at the new constructor.


