`backoff.RetryError` stutters at the call site, and the package returns exactly one error type, so it needs no qualifier. It also frees the `RetryError` name for a function — see #1, which uses it for the result-less `Retry` variant and is stacked on this branch.

`AsRetryError` goes with it. Nothing in the library used it; it only saved callers a variable declaration over `errors.As`:

```go
// before
if re := backoff.AsRetryError(err); re != nil {
	log.Printf("gave up after last error: %v", re.LastErr)
}

// after
var re *backoff.Error
if errors.As(err, &re) {
	log.Printf("gave up after last error: %v", re.LastErr)
}
```

`errors.As` is what Go callers reach for anyway, and `Error`'s doc comment already flags the reason it matters here — `Error` implements `Unwrap() []error`, so single-value `errors.Unwrap` returns nil and `errors.Is`/`errors.As` are the only way in.

Otherwise a pure rename: fields (`LastErr`, `Cause`), methods (`Error`, `Unwrap`), cause sentinels and all behaviour are unchanged.

**Breaking:** `*backoff.RetryError` → `*backoff.Error`; `backoff.AsRetryError` removed.

