## Summary

Adds the next extraction increment for the OpenOBSW/OpenSVF reference adapter: an internal **Resolved Projection Model** that sits between validated Core/Profile inputs and target artifact generators.

The new boundary is deliberately adapter-owned and candidate-only:

```text
Core Integration Input Set
        +
Projection Profile
        ↓
validate-profile
        ↓
resolve-projection
        ↓
Resolved Projection Model (adapter IR)
        ↓
future C / SRDB materializers
```

## Architectural purpose

This prevents target generators from becoming the place where source resolution, defaulting, naming, type interpretation and provenance decisions accumulate.

The resolver consumes only:

- `integration_input_manifest.json` and its Core-owned surfaces;
- the version-controlled OpenOBSW/OpenSVF Projection Profile;
- the packaged Profile JSON Schema.

It does **not** read Mission Model YAML.

The IR is intentionally not declared a generic Core or Studio contract. It is an internal adapter boundary that may evolve while the generic Integration Result remains the external normalized result surface.

## Resolved model

Identity:

- `kind: orbitfabric.openobsw_opensvf.resolved_projection`
- `resolved_projection_version: 0.1-candidate`

The model carries:

- integration/profile/mission identity;
- Core Input Set identity and digest;
- resolved integration settings;
- deterministic projections;
- explicit authored exclusions (`do_not_project`);
- Core-owned semantic payloads;
- resolved target values;
- per-value provenance for target/default decisions.

## Provenance rules

The resolver makes ownership explicit rather than relying on JSON location conventions:

- Core semantic payloads carry `origin: core`;
- Profile-authored target values carry `origin: profile`;
- deterministic adapter defaults carry `origin: adapter_default`.

Examples exercised by the real PoC profile:

- telemetry `unit` / `type` come from Core;
- telemetry `numeric_id` comes from Profile;
- omitted telemetry `srdb_name` defaults to the Core ID;
- omitted `c_symbol` is deterministically derived from the configured prefix + Core ID;
- command PUS / verification intent comes from Profile;
- event severity remains Core-owned;
- housekeeping packet membership and member telemetry semantics come from Core.

## Determinism

Profile binding order has no implicit semantics. The resolved projection and exclusion sets are therefore ordered deterministically by binding identity. A regression test verifies that reversing Profile binding order yields the same resolved model.

## CLI

Adds:

```bash
orbitfabric-openobsw-opensvf resolve-projection \
  --input-set-manifest <integration_input_manifest.json> \
  --profile <profile.yaml> \
  --json <resolved_projection.json>
```

Resolution always performs Profile/Input validation first. There is no bypass path for invalid inputs.

## Tests

New tests cover:

- Core semantics vs Profile target values vs adapter defaults;
- deterministic C-symbol derivation;
- SRDB defaulting;
- housekeeping packet membership + member telemetry expansion;
- command and event semantics;
- explicit `do_not_project` exclusions;
- invalid Profile rejection before resolution;
- binding-order independence;
- deterministic JSON serialization.

## Real Core handshake

The existing CI handshake is extended to prove:

```text
real PoC Mission Model
→ pinned validated OrbitFabric Core producer
→ real coherent Integration Input Set
→ real PoC Projection Profile validation
→ real resolved Projection Model
→ continuity assertions
```

The latest branch head `c5a81c7bea91117140c49c413c7e51406d6abe63` passed:

- adapter tests on Python 3.10;
- adapter tests on Python 3.11;
- adapter tests on Python 3.12;
- real Core Input Set export;
- real Profile validation;
- real Projection resolution;
- resolved continuity checks.

## Not included

This PR deliberately does not yet add:

- `mission_contract.h` generation;
- OpenSVF SRDB generation;
- Integration Result generation;
- runtime/campaign orchestration;
- Studio-specific behavior.

Those can now consume this resolved boundary rather than reconstructing semantics independently.

