#### Checklist

- [x] I have read the [coding conventions](https://github.com/LostArtefacts/TRX/blob/develop/docs/CONTRIBUTING.md#coding-conventions)
- [x] I have added a changelog entry about what my pull request accomplishes, or it is an internal change

#### Description

Objects now use a single key, and store their names in the game string table in a single `"name"` entry including aliases.

- The key is the lowercase C name without `O_`: `O_SHOTGUN_ITEM` becomes `shotgun_item`. The same key is used in strings, game flow, and weapon definitions.

- Names are expressed with plain strings keyed by `objects/<key>/name`; what used to be arrays is now just a string with `|` separators.
    ```json
    {
        "large_medipack_item": {
            "name": "Large Medipack|Big Medipack"
        }
    ```

- An object can reuse another object's name, so inventory options can inherit the item name they represent, without requiring duplicate translations.
    ```json
    "objects": {
        "pistols_item": {
            "name": "Pistols"
        },
        "pistols_option": {
            "name": "$objects/pistols_item/name"
        }
    }
    ```

- Object names now work like any other game string: scripts can read them with `trx.locale.get`, and references resolve through the normal string system.

    ```lua
    trx.locale.get("objects/large_medipack_item/name")  -- "Large Medipack|Big Medipack"
    trx.objects[trx.catalog.objects.large_medipack_item].name  -- {"Large Medipack"}
    trx.objects[trx.catalog.objects.large_medipack_item].names  -- {"Large Medipack", "Big Medipack"}
    trx.objects[trx.catalog.objects.pistols_option].names  -- { "Pistols" }
    ```

For players, nothing changes: the same objects go by the same names.
For mod authors, many string fields were renamed; see `docs/trx/MIGRATING.md` for the renamed keys and new name fields.

`object.names` is unchanged. `object.default_names` is now empty for inventory options, since they inherit names from the item they represent.

#### Testing

- [x] Inventory shows the right name for every pickup, weapon and option in each game.
- [x] The examine screen shows the right name and description.
- [x] `/give big medipack` in TR2 still gives Lara a large medipack.
- [x] Names follow the language chosen in the options, and change straight away when it is changed.

## Purpose

Add the backend procurement-engineering contract used by Discover custom acquisition, on top of the **actual deployed backend lineage** rather than stale `main`.

This is a **draft**. Do not merge or deploy yet.

## Base / lineage

Base: `live/deployed-backend-20260825` (`687529d7a9c7963e65b7d0e306467187417ac8c7`).

The final review diff is deliberately limited to three product files:

- `drive/scripts/research_data_mcp/procurement_execution_contract.py`
- `drive/scripts/research_data_mcp/craft_collect.py` (8 additive integration lines)
- `tests/test_procurement_execution_contract.py`

The earlier exploratory compiler branch was discovered to be based on stale `main`; this PR re-homes only the verified product changes onto deployed ancestry and excludes its docs / trigger / patching scaffolds.

## Contract

Discover may identify a source and choose a generic acquisition primitive, but the model does not own concrete runtime placement.

The compiler turns a semantic generic collect plan into a bounded runtime contract with:

- normalized required capabilities;
- bounded resource requirements;
- bounded shard / fan-out hints;
- bounded per-node concurrency;
- clamped retry policy;
- runtime-owned placement rather than model-selected worker/pool binding;
- explicit sizing basis;
- explicit preflight requirements or recommendations when important facts remain unmeasured;
- a compact backend-authored `engineering_summary` for researcher-facing UI;
- post-acquisition evidence reassessment authority.

The intended loop is:

**compile → identify uncertainty → preflight / measure → recompile → execute → reassess evidence fit**

## Authority boundaries

- compiler requirements ≠ worker assignment;
- baseline sizing ≠ measured sizing;
- preflight recommended / required ≠ execution approval;
- a compiled plan ≠ approved job;
- runtime placement remains owned by fresh cluster state;
- worker IDs, leases, reservations, and runtime topology are not model-authored;
- crafted acquisition continues to use generic primitives (`http_manifest`, `scraper_run`, `source_probe`) rather than named vendor product pipelines.

## Integration

`craft_collect.py` invokes compilation only after the existing generic plan has passed `validate_generic_plan`. This preserves the current craft doctrine and then adds runtime engineering authority before the plan is returned to Discover.

Frontend PR `Spectating101/yzu-cluster#82` consumes only the compiler's compact engineering summary and intentionally hides worker identity / reservation / contract-hash / DAG plumbing from the normal research UI.

## Validation

A temporary read-only certification workflow was run on this deployed-lineage branch and removed afterward. It passed:

- the procurement compiler contract suite;
- existing generic craft contracts;
- YZU cluster resource-placement tests;
- YZU cluster reliability tests;
- Python compilation for the touched modules;
- explicit ancestry checks proving the branch descends from deployed SHA `687529d…`.

The final branch is zero commits behind the deployed base and the final diff contains only the three product files above.

## Boundary

This PR is independent from the active Synthesis backend hardening branch / PR. No Synthesis implementation is modified here.

## Objetivo
Primeira atualização de produto do Guardei para reduzir atrito ao salvar links e tornar o acervo mais ativo, inteligente e preparado para evolução como SaaS, sem pagamentos nesta fase.

## Implementado
- sugestões automáticas de links detectados no clipboard, respeitando permissões do navegador;
- salvamento em um toque com classificação e deduplicação existentes;
- novo motor de recomendação contextual por prioridade, idade, status, esforço, duração, histórico e horário;
- lembretes inteligentes e bem-humorados do Guardinho;
- Guardinho com ações seguras: salvar, recomendar, marcar visto, arquivar, destacar, rever, categorizar e organizar Inbox;
- fallback para o chat de IA já existente quando a mensagem não é uma ação;
- Web Share Target preservado e fluxo de PWA melhorado;
- preferências inteligentes persistidas por usuário;
- testes automatizados do motor inteligente e CI.

## Web Push mobile concluído
- Web Push server-driven com VAPID usando `web-push`;
- subscription por dispositivo salva no PostgreSQL (`PushSubscription`);
- endpoint para chave pública, subscribe, unsubscribe, status e push de teste;
- Service Worker recebe evento `push` e mostra notificações mesmo com o app fechado;
- ações nas notificações para abrir o link, abrir o app ou marcar conteúdo como visto;
- renovação de subscription via `pushsubscriptionchange` + ressincronização no próximo foco;
- bootstrap no frontend que mantém o dispositivo inscrito quando `Lembretes` está ativo;
- subscription do dispositivo removida antes do logout para evitar notificações de uma conta anterior;
- scheduler interno no backend com frequência por usuário;
- endpoint protegido `POST /api/push/cron` para infraestrutura que dorme/escala para zero;
- subscriptions expiradas (404/410) são removidas automaticamente;
- comando `npm --prefix server run push:keys` para gerar as chaves VAPID;
- `server/.env.example` e documentação de configuração adicionados.

## Banco
O schema agora inclui `PushSubscription` e `lastSmartPushAt`. O fluxo existente de deploy usa `prisma db push`, portanto os novos campos/tabela entram pelo processo atual.

## Validação
- branch criada a partir da `main` e permanece sem divergência da base;
- PR mergeável;
- GitHub Actions executado no head final;
- instalação do frontend: sucesso;
- instalação do backend com `web-push`: sucesso;
- Prisma generate: sucesso;
- syntax check do backend/push: sucesso;
- testes automatizados: sucesso;
- build Vite: sucesso.

## Configuração necessária em produção
Gerar as chaves VAPID uma única vez e configurar `VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY`, `VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY` e `VAPID_SUBJECT` no ambiente do backend. `PUSH_SCHEDULER_ENABLED` fica ativo por padrão; `PUSH_CRON_SECRET` permite usar um cron externo opcional.

