## Outcome

Introduces the first non-Git organisational transport for Rack practice: one inspectable `.rack.yaml` file that can flow through the existing Rack compiler without a second product or compiler path.

## Why

Shared organisational practice should not require:

- Git;
- a terminal;
- a Rack account;
- a central Rack control plane.

A file can be distributed through OneDrive, Google Drive, Dropbox, Nextcloud, a shared network folder, email or chat.

## Implemented

### Shared file contract

- strict `rack.shared-practice` YAML envelope;
- document ID/version/title/publisher/licence metadata;
- inline canonical Rack instructions;
- publisher controls instruction authority;
- receiver controls source ID, relationship and precedence.

### Materialisation

- pure YAML → `PracticeSource` + `RackModule[]` + resolver candidates;
- original file path and document version retained as source provenance;
- deterministic synthetic module paths;
- Node host reader for an explicitly attached file path.

### Atomic safety

The whole source is blocked for:

- malformed YAML;
- invalid envelope;
- invalid Rack module;
- duplicate module IDs;
- published `local-only` practice;
- a binding instruction without a rationale;
- invalid receiver-owned source metadata.

No valid subset is silently applied.

### Update comparison

Incoming versions are classified as added/removed/changed, with a separate tightening signal for:

- new binding instructions;
- new required instructions;
- adaptable → binding;
- increased criticality;
- binding review date removed;
- binding review date pushed further out.

This comparison does not automatically accept or apply updates.

### Resolved Set-ups

Local Rack modules and shared candidates are combined through the Iteration 16 resolver before compilation.

- local practice remains nearest for adaptable conflicts;
- applicable winning shared bindings are added to the resolved Set-up even when the local profile never included their IDs;
- a local exclusion cannot suppress an applicable binding shared instruction in the resolved build;
- the local source file remains unchanged;
- new adaptable shared instructions are not silently auto-injected yet.

The resolved project then goes through the existing compiler/destination path.

